# Project Rules: be-github-tracking

## Architecture Principles

### DDD (Domain-Driven Design)
- Bounded contexts: Auth, Identity, GitHub Tracking
- Rich domain models with business logic in aggregates
- Repository pattern: Interface in domain, implementation in infrastructure
- Domain services for complex business logic spanning multiple aggregates

### CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)
- Commands: Write operations (Create, Update, Delete)
- Queries: Read operations (Find, Get, List)
- Handlers: One handler per command/query
- Cross-context communication: ONLY via CommandBus/QueryBus

### Bounded Context Rules
- ✅ Auth context can use QueryBus/CommandBus to Identity context
- ❌ Auth context CANNOT inject IUserRepository directly
- ✅ Each context manages its own repositories
- ✅ Use forwardRef() to prevent circular dependencies

## Code Standards

### Transaction Management
- Use @Transactional() decorator for multi-step operations
- Single DB operations don't need @Transactional()
- All handlers with multiple DB writes MUST be transactional

### Exception Handling
- Domain layer: Use domain exceptions (extend DomainException)
- Application layer: Can use NestJS exceptions
- Never use NestJS exceptions in domain aggregates
- Domain exceptions: TokenRevokedException, ValidationException, etc.

### Repository Pattern
```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
// Domain layer: Interface
export interface IUserRepository {
  findById(id: UserId): Promise<UserAggregate | null>;
}

// Infrastructure layer: Implementation
@Injectable()
export class PrismaUserRepository implements IUserRepository {
  // Prisma-specific code here
}
```

### Handler Pattern
```typescript
@CommandHandler(SomeCommand)
export class SomeHandler implements ICommandHandler<SomeCommand> {
  constructor(
    private readonly queryBus: QueryBus,
    private readonly commandBus: CommandBus,
    @Inject(ISomeRepository) private readonly repo: ISomeRepository,
  ) {}

  @Transactional() // If multi-step operation
  async execute(command: SomeCommand): Promise<Result> {
    // Implementation
  }
}
```

## Database Rules

### Prisma
- Use UUIDs for all primary keys (gen_random_uuid())
- Soft delete pattern: deleted_at, deleted_by_id
- Audit fields: created_at, created_by_id, updated_at, updated_by_id
- Use @map() for snake_case database columns
- Use @@map() for table names

### Migrations
- Always review migrations before applying
- Test migrations on development database first
- Never modify existing migrations
- Use descriptive migration names

## Security Rules

### Authentication
- Token rotation on refresh
- Revocation support (single session & all sessions)
- Device tracking for refresh tokens
- Expiration validation

### Authorization
- Role-based access control (ADMIN, LECTURER, STUDENT)
- Use @Roles() decorator for endpoints
- RolesGuard for role checking
- Default role for new OAuth users: STUDENT

### Data Protection
- No sensitive data in logs
- No passwords in plain text
- Hash sensitive data before storage
- Sanitize error messages in production

## Testing Standards

### Unit Tests
- Test domain logic in aggregates
- Test business rules
- Mock external dependencies
- Aim for >80% coverage

### Integration Tests
- Test handlers with real database
- Test CQRS flow
- Test transaction rollback

### E2E Tests
- Test critical user flows
- Test authentication flow
- Test authorization

## Performance

### Database Optimization
- Use indexes on frequently queried fields
- Avoid N+1 queries
- Use select only needed fields
- Batch operations when possible

### Caching (Future)
- Cache user data (5 minutes)
- Cache role permissions
- Invalidate on updates

## Git Workflow

### Commit Messages
- Format: `type(scope): message`
- Types: feat, fix, refactor, test, docs, chore
- Example: `feat(auth): add Google OAuth login`

### Branch Naming
- Format: `type/description`
- Example: `feat/google-oauth`, `fix/token-expiration`

## Documentation

### Code Comments
- Document WHY, not WHAT
- Use JSDoc for public APIs
- Document complex business logic
- Keep comments up-to-date

### API Documentation
- Use Swagger decorators
- Document all endpoints
- Include request/response examples
- Document error codes
